## 12. Integrations

### 12.1 Opportunity Sources

The system should support an extensible integration strategy for:

- Greenhouse and Lever job listings or supported integrations.
- Licensed job and opportunity feeds.
- Public job and opportunity APIs.
- Company career pages where access is permitted.
- Supported external opportunity platforms.
- User-submitted URLs.

Each source should have its own ingestion, normalization, error-handling, and monitoring logic.

The availability of an integration must not be assumed until its access method, permissions, and technical requirements are verified.

### 12.2 AI Provider

The platform should use an AI service abstraction to support model changes, provider evaluation, structured outputs, and cost management.

### 12.3 Email and Notifications

An email or notification provider should support account-related messages, application reminders, and user-configured alerts.

### 12.4 Document Services

The platform should support secure processing and generation of common career-document formats.

### 12.5 Browser-Assisted Automation

Browser automation may be added for supported application workflows where legally and technically appropriate.

Such automation must respect site terms, authentication requirements, user consent, and approval controls.

## 13. Security, Privacy, and Compliance

Security and privacy are foundational product requirements.

### 13.1 Authentication and Authorization

- Use a secure authentication mechanism.
- Enforce server-side authorization for sensitive actions.
- Apply PostgreSQL Row Level Security where appropriate.
- Prevent users from accessing other users' career profiles, documents, and applications.
- Protect administrative functions with appropriate role-based permissions.

### 13.2 Document Security

- Store private documents in private storage.
- Use appropriately scoped, short-lived signed URLs where needed.
- Validate file types and enforce upload limits.
- Prevent unauthorized document access.
- Apply secure processing controls to uploaded files.

### 13.3 Secrets and Credentials

- Keep API keys and integration secrets on the server side.
- Never expose privileged credentials in frontend code.
- Use secure environment configuration and appropriate secret management.
- Avoid storing third-party account passwords unless a specific, justified, secure integration requires it.

### 13.4 User Consent and Approval

- Obtain explicit approval before supported external application submissions.
- Make automation permissions understandable and revocable.
- Keep a record of relevant approvals and actions.
- Do not treat consent to prepare an application as consent to submit it.

### 13.5 Data Rights and Retention

Users should have appropriate ways to access, export, correct, and request deletion of their personal information.

Retention and deletion policies should cover career documents, extracted information, generated materials, logs, and connected integrations, subject to applicable legal requirements.

### 13.6 External Content and Abuse Prevention

The platform must protect against prompt injection, malicious links, unsafe file uploads, unauthorized tool calls, and misuse of integrations.

### 13.7 Compliance Review

Before launching in relevant markets, review applicable data protection, privacy, employment, consumer protection, intellectual property, and website-access requirements.

## 14. Non-Functional Requirements

### 14.1 Performance

- Common dashboard and profile reads should target a p95 response time under two seconds under representative operating conditions, excluding external-provider latency.
- Long-running AI and document-processing tasks should run asynchronously where appropriate.
- Search and filtering should remain responsive as opportunity records grow.
- Expensive operations should use caching or other optimizations where justified.

### 14.2 Reliability

- The system should implement retries and error handling for transient failures.
- Background jobs should be observable and recoverable.
- Duplicate job processing should not create duplicate applications or other unintended side effects.
- The production service should target 99.5% availability after the pilot, subject to the agreed service definition and operational arrangements.

### 14.3 Scalability

- The data model should support growth in users, opportunity records, documents, and application activity.
- Opportunity-source integrations should scale independently where practical.
- AI usage should be monitored and controlled through appropriate rate limits and cost controls.

### 14.4 Accessibility

The interface should target WCAG 2.2 AA accessibility practices, including keyboard navigation, readable contrast, accessible form labels, and appropriate screen-reader support.

### 14.5 Maintainability

- Use clear module boundaries and documented interfaces.
- Maintain automated tests for critical business logic.
- Keep configuration separate from source code.
- Support reliable schema migrations.
- Document integration setup, operational procedures, and failure recovery.

### 14.6 Security Quality

No unresolved critical or high-severity security defects should remain at launch.

